{:edb/db-before-t 1463 :edb/db-after-t 1464 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534776 50 #inst "2026-10-08T06:37:19.350Z" 13194139534776 true] [17592186147284 1100 "The intersection of the planes $x+2y+4z=0$ and $2x+4y+2z+18=0$ is given by the line\n\n$\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 0, a_2, a_3\\rangle + t \\mathbf b, \\quad t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nCalculate $a_2+a_3$, and find a possible direction vector $\\mathbf b$." 13194139534776 false] [17592186147284 1100 "The intersection of the planes $x+2y+4z=0$ and $2x+4y+2z+18=0$ is given by the line \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 0, a_2, a_3\\rangle + t \\mathbf b, \\quad t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nCalculate $a_2+a_3,$ and find a possible direction vector $\\mathbf b.$" 13194139534776 true] [17592186147284 1145 "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $x=0$. Substituting this into the first equation gives\n\n$$\n0+2y+4z=0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad y=-2z\n$$\n\nand substituting $x=0$ and $y=-2z$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + 4y + 2z + 18 &= 0 \\\\\n2(0) + 4(-2z) + 2z + 18 &= 0 \\\\\n- 6z &= -18 \\\\\nz &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-2z=-6$ and\n\n$$\n\\mathbf a = \\langle 0,-6,3\\rangle\n$$\n\nis a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+2y+4z=0$, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 1,2,4 \\rangle$.\n- For the plane $2x+4y+2z+18=0$, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle 2,4,2 \\rangle$.\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn_{1} \\cdot n_{2} &= ⟨1, 2, 4⟩ \\cdot ⟨2, 4, 2⟩ \\\\\n&= i\\begin{vmatrix}j & k \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}4 \\\\\n&= 2 \\mid 4 \\mid 2 \\\\\n&=-12i + 6j \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 12, 6, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can pick any vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2$ as a direction vector of the line. So, let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} =\\dfrac{1}{6}(\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2) = \\langle -2, 1, 0 \\rangle\n$$\n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= a + tb \\\\\nr(t) &= ⟨0,-6, 3⟩ + t⟨ - 2, 1, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\na_2 +a_3 = -6+3 = -3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf b = \\langle -2,1,0\\rangle\n$$" 13194139534776 false] [17592186147284 1145 "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $x=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation gives   \n\n$$\n0+2y+4z=0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad y=-2z,\n$$\n\nand substituting $x=0$ and $y=-2z$ into the second equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+4y+2z+18 & =0 \\\\ 2(0)+4(-2z)+2z+18 & =0 \\\\ -6z & =-18 \\\\ z & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-2z=-6$ and $\\mathbf a = \\langle 0,-6,3\\rangle$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+2y+4z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 1,2,4 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $2x+4y+2z+18=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle 2,4,2 \\rangle.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,2,4⟩\\times ⟨2,4,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-12i+6j \\\\  & =⟨-12,6,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can pick any vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2$ as a direction vector of the line. So, let \n \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} =\\dfrac{1}{6}(\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2) = \\langle -2, 1, 0 \\rangle.\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨0,-6,3⟩+t⟨-2,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, $a_2 +a_3 = -6+3 = -3,$ and $\\mathbf b = \\langle  -2,1,0\\rangle.$" 13194139534776 true] [17592186147285 1100 "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+4y+5=0 \\\\ 3y-2z-7=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534776 true] [17592186147285 1100 "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n${2x + 4y + 5 = 0; 3y - 2z - 7 = 0$" 13194139534776 false] [17592186147285 1145 "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x+4y +5= 0$, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 2,4,0 \\rangle$.\n- For the plane $3y-2z-7= 0$, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle 0,3,-2 \\rangle$.\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv &= n_{1} \\cdot n_{2} \\\\\n&= ⟨2, 4, 0⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, 3,-2⟩ \\\\\n&= i\\begin{vmatrix}j & k \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix}0 \\\\\n&= 0 \\mid 3 \\mid - 2 \\\\\n&=-8i + 4j + 6k \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 8, 4, 6⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534776 false] [17592186147285 1145 "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x+4y +5= 0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 2,4,0 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $3y-2z-7= 0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle 0,3,-2 \\rangle.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,4,0⟩\\times ⟨0,3,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\,i & \\,j & \\,k\\, \\\\ \\,2 & \\,4 & \\,0\\, \\\\ \\,0 & \\,3 & \\,\\,\\,-2\\,\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-8i+4j+6k \\\\  & =⟨-8,4,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534776 true] [17592186147286 1100 "Given the two planes $4x-2y=0$ and $-2x+y+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $\\mathbf{0}.$\n\n- The planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\n- The planes intersect on a straight line." 13194139534776 true] [17592186147286 1100 "Given the two planes $4x-2y=0$ and $-2x+y+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $\\mathbf{0}$.\n2. The planes are parallel but not coincident.\n3. The planes intersect on a straight line." 13194139534776 false] [17592186147286 1145 "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $4x-2y=0$, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 4,-2,0 \\rangle$.\n- For the plane $-2x+y+1=0$, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle -2,1,0 \\rangle$.\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n$n_{1} \\cdot n_{2}|= ⟨4,-2, 0⟩ \\cdot ⟨ - 2, 1, 0⟩; n|= i\\begin{vmatrix}j & k \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix}0; - 2 \\mid 1 \\mid 0; = 0$.\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2 =\\mathbf{0}$, the vectors $\\mathbf{n}_1$ and $\\mathbf{n}_2$ are parallel. However, the first plane passes through the origin $O(0,0,0)$ but the second plane doesn't: $-2(0)+(0)+1=1 \\neq 0$ So, the planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the planes are parallel but not coincident, they cannot intersect each other.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." 13194139534776 false] [17592186147286 1145 "First, notice that: \n\n- For the plane $4x-2y=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 4,-2,0 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $-2x+y+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle -2,1,0 \\rangle.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨4,-2,0⟩\\times ⟨-2,1,0⟩ \\\\ n & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2 =\\mathbf{0}$, the vectors $\\mathbf{n}_1$ and $\\mathbf{n}_2$ are parallel. However,  the first plane passes through the origin  $O(0,0,0)$ but the second plane doesn't: \n\n$$\n-2(0)+(0)+1=1 \\neq 0\n$$\n\n So, the planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the planes are parallel but not coincident, they cannot intersect each other.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." 13194139534776 true] [17592186147366 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309477 13194139534776 true] [17592186147366 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309484 13194139534776 true] [17592186147366 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309533 13194139534776 true] [17592186147366 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309540 13194139534776 true] [17592186147367 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309470 13194139534776 true] [17592186147367 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309505 13194139534776 true] [17592186147367 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309512 13194139534776 true] [17592186147367 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309519 13194139534776 true] [17592186147367 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309526 13194139534776 true] [17592186147368 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309491 13194139534776 true] [17592186147368 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309498 13194139534776 true] [17592186147368 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309547 13194139534776 true] [17592186147368 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309554 13194139534776 true] [17592186148144 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534776 true] [17592186148144 1100 "Given the two planes $3x - y + z = 0$ and $x - z + 4 = 0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0$.\n2. The planes are parallel.\n3. The planes intersect on a line." 13194139534776 false] [17592186148144 1100 "Given the two planes $3x-y+z=0$ and $x-z+4=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$\n- The planes are parallel.\n- The planes intersect on a line." 13194139534776 true] [17592186148144 1145 "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $3x-y+z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,-1,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-z+4=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨3,-1,1⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i+4j+k \\\\  & =⟨1,4,1⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. As a result, the planes are not parallel either.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." 13194139534776 true] [17592186148147 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534776 true] [17592186148147 1100 "The intersection of the planes $-x + y - 2z = 0$ and $-x - 2y + z - 3 = 0$ is given by the line\n\n$r(t) = ⟨a_{1}, a_{2}, 0⟩ + tb, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$.\n\nCalculate $a_{1} + a_{2}$, and find a possible direction vector $b$." 13194139534776 false] [17592186148147 1100 "The intersection of the planes $-x+y-2z=0$ and $-x-2y+z-3=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{2},0⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" 13194139534776 true] [17592186148147 1145 "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n-x+y-2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,x=y,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ and $x=y$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x-2y+z-3 & =0 \\\\ -y-2y+0-3 & =0 \\\\ -3y & =3 \\\\ y & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=y=-1$ and $a=⟨-1,-1,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $-x+y-2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨-1,1,-2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $-x-2y+z-3=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨-1,-2,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨-1,1,-2⟩\\times ⟨-1,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-3i+3j+3k \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can pick any vector that is parallel to ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}$ as a direction vector of the line. So, let \n\n$$\nb=\\frac{1}{3}({n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2})=⟨-1,1,1⟩.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨-1,-1,0⟩+t⟨-1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-1-1=-2,$ and $b=⟨-1,1,1⟩.$" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309470 1017 #edb/ref 17592186309471 13194139534776 true] [17592186309470 1089 "q-129170" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309470 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534776 true] [17592186309470 1100 "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3x+4z-11=0 \\\\ x-2y+31=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309470 1113 #uuid "2f626f64-b605-58e9-8e55-f56146c3feb3" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309470 1145 "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $-3x+4z-11=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨-3,0,4⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-2y+31=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-2,0⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨-3,0,4⟩\\times ⟨1,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =8i+4j+6k \\\\  & =⟨8,4,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309471 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309472 13194139534776 true] [17592186309471 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309473 13194139534776 true] [17592186309471 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309474 13194139534776 true] [17592186309471 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309475 13194139534776 true] [17592186309471 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309476 13194139534776 true] [17592186309471 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534776 true] [17592186309471 1041 "selection" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309471 1104 #edb/ref 17592186309475 13194139534776 true] [17592186309471 1110 #uuid "0daf3085-8d89-5e14-92e1-e117adc2c4ab" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309472 1034 "⟨4,8,6⟩" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309472 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534776 true] [17592186309472 1111 #uuid "f6faec47-b0e1-53cc-a122-ba924137a87c" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309473 1034 "⟨4,0,3⟩" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309473 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534776 true] [17592186309473 1111 #uuid "239ff84d-7ca6-561a-8043-85e62e7c8a24" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309474 1034 "⟨4,4,6⟩" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309474 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534776 true] [17592186309474 1111 #uuid "79bb4a2d-dafd-5be4-af5a-4aa992b2f677" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309475 1034 "⟨8,4,6⟩" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309475 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534776 true] [17592186309475 1111 #uuid "987c5ac6-e52d-5391-9b0f-a25f3e33e71e" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309476 1034 "⟨4,2,0⟩" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309476 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534776 true] [17592186309476 1111 #uuid "f344b47d-af36-5ae8-8c3d-e30f4f54e1cc" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309477 1017 #edb/ref 17592186309478 13194139534776 true] [17592186309477 1089 "q-129202" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309477 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534776 true] [17592186309477 1100 "The intersection of the planes $3x-2y-18=0$ and $2y-3z+18=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},0,{a}_{3}⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}-{a}_{3},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309477 1113 #uuid "b183e73c-612e-556a-a6fa-cdffff7f175f" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309477 1145 "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $y=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives \n\n$$\n3x-0-18=0\\,⟹\\,x=6,\n$$\n\nand substituting $y=0$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n0-3z+18=0\\,⟹\\,z=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=⟨6,0,6⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $3x-2y-18=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,-2,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2y-3z+18=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,2,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,0⟩\\times ⟨0,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =6i+9j+6k \\\\  & =⟨6,9,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨6,0,6⟩+t⟨6,9,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=6-6=0,$ and $b=⟨6,9,6⟩.$" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309478 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309479 13194139534776 true] [17592186309478 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309480 13194139534776 true] [17592186309478 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309481 13194139534776 true] [17592186309478 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309482 13194139534776 true] [17592186309478 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309483 13194139534776 true] [17592186309478 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534776 true] [17592186309478 1041 "selection" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309478 1104 #edb/ref 17592186309483 13194139534776 true] [17592186309478 1110 #uuid "a533e508-2e7f-507e-9ebe-7c8ad24094f6" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309479 1034 "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=3,\\,b=⟨6,3,6⟩" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309479 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534776 true] [17592186309479 1111 #uuid "e009f408-0948-583c-9f36-aa61e528c12c" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309480 1034 "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=0,\\,b=⟨3,9,3⟩" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309480 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534776 true] [17592186309480 1111 #uuid "d5418a96-3eea-52a4-99cc-cba6ec5608b0" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309481 1034 "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=-3,\\,b=⟨9,6,6⟩" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309481 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534776 true] [17592186309481 1111 #uuid "99a54632-3487-5f66-b6f7-4f9fe3d532de" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309482 1034 "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=6,\\,b=⟨6,6,3⟩" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309482 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534776 true] [17592186309482 1111 #uuid "154c79d3-6bc5-5eb8-9dc0-6d20092039a8" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309483 1034 "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=0,\\,b=⟨6,9,6⟩" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309483 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534776 true] [17592186309483 1111 #uuid "cf84f720-68bf-5be5-8c1f-4f6989161d6c" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309484 1017 #edb/ref 17592186309485 13194139534776 true] [17592186309484 1089 "q-129224" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309484 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534776 true] [17592186309484 1100 "The intersection of the planes $2x-y-3z-8=0$ and $x+2y-3z-4=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{2},0⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309484 1113 #uuid "923148c0-0ecb-5f91-a025-b18771e50157" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309484 1145 "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n2x-y-3(0)-8=0\\,⟹\\,y=2x-8,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ and $y=2x-8$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2(2x-8)-0-4 & =0 \\\\ x+4x-16-4 & =0 \\\\ 5x & =20 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=2(4)-8=0$ and $a=⟨4,0,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x-y-3z-8=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-1,-3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x+2y-3z-4=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,2,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨2,-1,-3⟩\\times ⟨1,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =9i+3j+5k \\\\  & =⟨9,3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨4,0,0⟩+t⟨9,3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=4+0=4,$ and $b=⟨9,3,5⟩.$" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309485 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309486 13194139534776 true] [17592186309485 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309487 13194139534776 true] [17592186309485 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309488 13194139534776 true] [17592186309485 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309489 13194139534776 true] [17592186309485 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309490 13194139534776 true] [17592186309485 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534776 true] [17592186309485 1041 "selection" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309485 1104 #edb/ref 17592186309488 13194139534776 true] [17592186309485 1110 #uuid "8149ef53-3f86-5b48-a7e1-8fa0afbc8615" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309486 1034 "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-4,\\,b=⟨9,5,3⟩" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309486 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534776 true] [17592186309486 1111 #uuid "7b41fa7c-383b-551d-8e7d-195af093e233" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309487 1034 "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=8,\\,b=⟨5,3,9⟩" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309487 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534776 true] [17592186309487 1111 #uuid "3bef7e40-b70b-5a85-8a24-375e22f4f302" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309488 1034 "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=4,\\,b=⟨9,3,5⟩" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309488 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534776 true] [17592186309488 1111 #uuid "6700ede1-a36b-5eee-942e-0d9f0f54f842" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309489 1034 "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=2,\\,b=⟨3,9,5⟩" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309489 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534776 true] [17592186309489 1111 #uuid "2f47da4a-b316-542d-9add-3c553c97e434" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309490 1034 "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-2,\\,b=⟨6,3,5⟩" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309490 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534776 true] [17592186309490 1111 #uuid "eca8e996-c8fa-5d8f-b034-7e46ddd5ba4d" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309491 1017 #edb/ref 17592186309492 13194139534776 true] [17592186309491 1089 "q-129239" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309491 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534776 true] [17592186309491 1100 "Given the two planes $2x-5y+z+1=0$ and $3x-z+7=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$\n- The planes are parallel.\n- The planes intersect on a line." 13194139534776 true] [17592186309491 1113 #uuid "8065d082-8b78-5fe6-a484-46d6c36cc283" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309491 1145 "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $2x-5y+z+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-5,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $3x-z+7=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,0,-1⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨2,-5,1⟩\\times ⟨3,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =5i+5j+15k \\\\  & =⟨5,5,15⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. As a result, the planes are not parallel either.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." 13194139534776 true] [17592186309492 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309493 13194139534776 true] [17592186309492 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309494 13194139534776 true] [17592186309492 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309495 13194139534776 true] [17592186309492 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309496 13194139534776 true] [17592186309492 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309497 13194139534776 true] [17592186309492 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534776 true] [17592186309492 1041 "selection" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309492 1104 #edb/ref 17592186309496 13194139534776 true] [17592186309492 1110 #uuid "43a6fea0-fd9c-5954-a8d9-c8f50d9254ea" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309493 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309493 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534776 true] [17592186309493 1111 #uuid "3d92abd1-6987-59aa-b974-08ee8d41ee28" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309494 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309494 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534776 true] [17592186309494 1111 #uuid "b1d6e9e6-5aba-52d3-8c81-15583f3aeb82" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309495 1034 "I only" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309495 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534776 true] [17592186309495 1111 #uuid "ee5c003f-4cef-5c77-8d0f-1488927948d0" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309496 1034 "III only" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309496 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534776 true] [17592186309496 1111 #uuid "855cd76b-6735-5058-9299-202e4e034510" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309497 1034 "II only" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309497 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534776 true] [17592186309497 1111 #uuid "a839178d-05a9-56fa-90eb-8225e7dba44e" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309498 1017 #edb/ref 17592186309499 13194139534776 true] [17592186309498 1089 "q-129259" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309498 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534776 true] [17592186309498 1100 "Given the two planes $2x-y+3z=0$ and $5x-2y+7=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨7,15,2⟩.$\n- The planes intersect on a line.\n- The planes are coincident." 13194139534776 true] [17592186309498 1113 #uuid "0531b19e-756e-5c97-abd0-1f5e648bd0cf" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309498 1145 "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $2x-y+3z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-1,3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $5x-2y+7=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨5,-2,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨2,-1,3⟩\\times ⟨5,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =6i+15j+k \\\\  & =⟨6,15,1⟩\\ne ⟨7,15,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. Hence, the planes must intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, two non-parallel planes cannot be coincident.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." 13194139534776 true] [17592186309499 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309500 13194139534776 true] [17592186309499 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309501 13194139534776 true] [17592186309499 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309502 13194139534776 true] [17592186309499 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309503 13194139534776 true] [17592186309499 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309504 13194139534776 true] [17592186309499 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534776 true] [17592186309499 1041 "selection" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309499 1104 #edb/ref 17592186309504 13194139534776 true] [17592186309499 1110 #uuid "cb6f1dbf-64e4-5fbc-ab2f-a0d761ede179" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309500 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309500 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534776 true] [17592186309500 1111 #uuid "f6bc9956-3f2a-5751-9955-380cc068e4be" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309501 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309501 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534776 true] [17592186309501 1111 #uuid "5cb4e7da-d423-537d-b6d1-eca94ff0ffac" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309502 1034 "III only" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309502 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534776 true] [17592186309502 1111 #uuid "fdd6c990-9990-5e92-9979-5092fd03374b" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309503 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534776 true] [17592186309503 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534776 true] [17592186309503 1111 #uuid "6a28c4a1-3a4b-5b47-8e2a-096224035b8c" 1319413